<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Donald Trump</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">b. <a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Donald Trump (born 1946) is an American businessman and Republican politician who served as the 45th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 2017 to 2021 and became the 47th president in January 2025. His administrations restricted immigration and raised tariffs; in his second term he demanded greater US control over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panama-canal">Panama Canal</a> and in late 2025 pardoned the former Honduran president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-orlando-hernandez">Juan Orlando Hernández</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/donald-trump.jpg" alt="Donald Trump" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Torok · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Presidential_Portrait_of_President_Donald_J._Trump_(2025).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…re-election in 2017 after the Supreme Court set aside the ban on a second term, in a disputed count followed by deadly protests; after leaving office he was extradited to the United States and convicted of drug trafficking in 2024, then pardoned by <b>Donald Trump</b> in late 2025.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-honduras">Republic of Honduras · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>…been its largest trading partner since 2013 and the United States a close partner in defence, and because it talks to both Washington and Pyongyang it hosted the first meeting of a sitting American president and a North Korean leader, <b>Donald Trump</b> and Kim Jong-un, in June 2018.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States the shifts in conservative thinking after the election of <b>Donald Trump</b> have been described as a “new fusionism” of traditional conservatism and right-wing populism, with more support for protectionism and a repudiation of neoconservatism.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/conservatism">Conservatism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Japan’s economic system is sometimes called neo-mercantilist; in 2007 Robert J. Samuelson wrote that China was pursuing an essentially neo-mercantilist trade policy, and after 2024 Branko Milanović described <b>Donald Trump</b>’s tariffs as “neo-mercantilism”.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/mercantilism">Mercantilism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Donald Trump</b> ran in 2016 on the slogans “Make America Great Again” and “America First”, and in 2018 declared himself a nationalist.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/nationalism">Nationalism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Jennifer Lopez sang it at the inauguration of Joe Biden in 2021 and Carrie Underwood at the second inauguration of <b>Donald Trump</b> in 2025.</blockquote><a href="/songs/america-the-beautiful">America the Beautiful</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Jamaica Hospital Medical Center, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.7,-73.82&amp;year=1981" aria-label="Jamaica Hospital Medical Center on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2741 710 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Donald Trump"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3191 980h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3191 980h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.7,-73.82&amp;year=1981">What was here in 1981? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
